<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jiaozhi Province (Ming)</h1><p class="st-meta">1407 CE – 1427 CE · capital Dong Quan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The twenty years of direct Ming Chinese rule over Vietnam after 1407, ended by Le Loi's ten-year war of resistance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ho-dynasty">Ho dynasty</a> in 1407, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> annexed the country as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi-province">Jiaozhi Province</a>, reviving an old Han name. The Ming administration was based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Dong Quan</a>, present-day Hanoi, under military and civil commissioners.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ming officials divided the province into prefectures and counties, imposed Chinese law, taxed salt, gold and forest products, and recruited labour and craftsmen for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. They removed books and historical records, promoted Chinese customs and schools, and conscripted local men. These policies provoked continuous rebellion, first under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tran-dynasty">Later Tran dynasty</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Le Loi</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lam-son">Lam Son</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Le Loi's</a> insurgency, begun in 1418, won major victories in 1426 and 1427, defeating Ming relief armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chi-lang-pass">Chi Lang</a>. The Ming agreed to withdraw in 1427, and in 1428 Le Loi proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty</a>. Ming rule was the last period of direct Chinese rule in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jiaozhi-province-ming.jpg" alt="Jiaozhi Province (Ming)" width="320" height="319"><figcaption>Image: Albert Herrmann &amp; Georg Westermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Domination_of_Vietnam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1407 CE – 1427 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dong Quan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhang Fu, Wang Tong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classical Chinese, Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ming Jiaozhi, Fourth Chinese domination of Vietnam, Giao Chỉ</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1417&amp;focus=jiaozhi-province-ming" aria-label="Jiaozhi Province (Ming) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7508 1367 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jiaozhi Province (Ming) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M7861 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7828 1623h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7828 1623h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Vietnam. <a href="/atlas?year=1417&amp;focus=jiaozhi-province-ming">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
